Glossary of Technical Terms & Acronyms
•	API (Application Programming Interface): A set of rules and protocols that allows different software systems or services to communicate and share data with each other automatically.
•	BaaS (Backend-as-a-Service): A cloud service model that provides developers with ready-to-use backend infrastructure (databases, authentication, file storage) so they don't have to build server architecture from scratch.
•	CDN (Content Delivery Network): A geographically distributed network of servers that caches website assets (images, code files) closer to users worldwide to deliver fast web page loading speeds.
•	CI/CD (Continuous Integration / Continuous Deployment): Automated software engineering workflows where code updates are automatically tested, built, and pushed to live production servers whenever changes are made.
•	CMS (Content Management System): A software platform (like WordPress) that lets users create, manage, and edit website content visually without manually writing HTML or backend code.
•	CRM (Customer Relationship Management): Software systems used to manage, track, and organize business interactions with prospective leads, clients, and user data.
•	DNS (Domain Name System): The internet's address book system that translates human-readable domain names (e.g., example.com) into computer IP addresses.
•	LLM (Large Language Model): Advanced AI models (such as Gemini or OpenAI GPT models) trained on vast datasets to understand, generate, and reason with natural human language and code.
•	OAuth (Open Authorization): An open standard protocol for secure access delegation (e.g., "Sign in with Google") that allows apps to verify user identity without directly accessing user passwords.
•	RAG (Retrieval-Augmented Generation): An AI technique where a model retrieves factual, relevant information from an external database or document repository before generating an answer, preventing hallucinations and ensuring high accuracy.
•	RLS (Row Level Security): A fine-grained database security strategy in PostgreSQL/Supabase that controls which specific rows of data individual users can view or modify based on their user roles.
•	SSL/TLS (Secure Sockets Layer / Transport Layer Security): Standard cryptographic protocols that encrypt data transmitted over the internet, establishing secure HTTPS connections between web browsers and servers.
•	UI (User Interface): The visual layout, buttons, menus, and forms that users directly view and interact with on a website or app screen.
